{
  "id": "Compound::DB01151 - Gene::1129",
  "verbalization": "Desipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant, is shown to bind to Chrm4, a receptor involved in the activation of G-protein coupled acetylcholine or neurotransmitter receptors. This binding leads to the participation of Chrm4 in activities such as G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity or neurotransmitter receptor activity, which in turn are associated with Chrm2. The evidence suggests that Desipramine may influence Chrm2 through a mechanism involving the activation of G-protein coupled receptors, potentially modulating the function of Chrm2 in cellular processes. The consistent involvement of Chrm4 in these pathways, despite the slight variation in the specific receptor activity types, indicates a general role in receptor-mediated signaling. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible mechanism by which Desipramine could interact with Chrm2, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
